From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 00:06:19 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] settings: add the per-profile "engine" setting (ADR 0007)

Flows through the existing pipe: settings JSON -> TerminalSettingsModel
-> IControlSettings -> the factory at pane creation in
ControlInteractivity. Only "cascadia" is valid today; it stays the
default indefinitely and ghostty will be opt-in per profile.

Unlike every other enum setting, an unrecognised value here must not
fail the settings load. The usual EnumMapper throws DeserializationError
and SettingsLoader rethrows it as a hard failure - an error dialog, with
none of the user's settings applied. "ghostty" is the value users will
reach for first and stays unrecognised until that engine ships, so a
typo-grade mistake would break the whole settings file.

So TerminalEngine gets a tolerant conversion trait that falls back to
cascadia, and Profile::LayerJson spots the fallback from the raw JSON so
CascadiaSettings can raise the new SettingsLoadWarnings::UnknownEngine.
This makes "engine" deliberately more permissive than its siblings; the
inconsistency is intentional and recorded for the retro.

ProfileTests::ProfileEngineSetting pins all three behaviours. Both
halves were mutation-tested: dropping _validateEnginesExist() fails the
warning assertion, and reverting the tolerant trait reproduces the hard
load failure ("* Line 16, Column 35 (engine)").

